VALUES (4, 'What is a computer network?', 'A. A single computer used by multiple people', 'B. A group of interconnected computers and devices that share resources and information', 'C. A collection of unrelated software programs', 'D. An isolated computer with no internet connection', 'B', 'A computer network is defined as a system of interconnected computers and hardware devices that communicate to share resources and information.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Sharing a printer over a network can save money and time for a company.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Not always', 'D. Only in small companies', 'A', 'Sharing devices like printers over a network reduces the number of devices needed and speeds up access, saving money and time.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT an advantage of using a computer network?', 'A. Sharing files easily among users', 'B. Increased cost due to multiple printers for each user', 'C. Sharing peripheral devices such as scanners', 'D. Collaboration through shared data access', 'B', 'Using a network actually reduces cost by sharing devices, so increased cost from multiple printers is not an advantage.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A computer that is not connected to any other computer is part of a network.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Sometimes', 'D. Only if it has an internet connection', 'B', 'A single, isolated computer without connection to others is not considered part of a network.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Which scenario best exemplifies a computer network?', 'A. A group of computers connected to share files and printers', 'B. Multiple computers each with their own isolated storage', 'C. One computer used by multiple users logging in sequentially', 'D. A computer connected only to the internet but no local devices', 'A', 'A network involves multiple interconnected computers sharing resources like files and printers.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: A network helps users share ______ such as printers, scanners, and files.', 'A. resources', 'B. passwords', 'C. internet connections', 'D. emails', 'A', 'Network resources refer to hardware and data shared among users in a network.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: A stand-alone computer is one that is ______ connected to any other computers or network.', 'A. not', 'B. always', 'C. sometimes', 'D. physically', 'A', 'A stand-alone computer operates independently and is not connected to other systems.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: A network allows at least one process in one device to ______ data to/from another process on a remote device.', 'A. send or receive', 'B. delete', 'C. encrypt', 'D. disconnect', 'A', 'The essential definition of a network includes the ability of processes to send and receive data between devices.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpret the following scenario:  ```A company has 20 employees and only 2 printers. Instead of buying a printer per employee, they connect the printers via a network. How does this setup benefit the company?```', 'A. It saves cost and allows employees to share the printers efficiently', 'B. It increases printer downtime', 'C. It isolates printer usage to specific employees only', 'D. It makes printers slower', 'A', 'Networked printers allow resource sharing, reducing costs and improving utilization among employees.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What defines the communication medium in a computer network?', 'A. The software applications used', 'B. The physical or wireless channels that connect devices', 'C. The data files stored on local disks', 'D. The number of users in the network', 'B', 'Communication medium refers to the physical or wireless channels that enable data transmission between devices.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of these statements correctly describes a network resource?', 'A. A stand-alone computer''s hard drive', 'B. Peripheral devices like printers and shared files accessible by network users', 'C. Internet browsers installed on a computer', 'D. Local software that cannot be accessed by others', 'B', 'Network resources include shared devices and data accessible by multiple users connected to the network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What must exist for two devices to be considered connected in a network?', 'A. At least one process on each device that can send or receive data from the other', 'B. They must be physically in the same room', 'C. Both devices must have the same operating system', 'D. They must be connected to the internet', 'A', 'Two devices are in a network if processes on each can communicate by sending/receiving data.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why does using a network generally save money compared to using standalone computers?', 'A. It reduces the number of devices needed by allowing sharing', 'B. It reduces internet costs', 'C. It eliminates software licensing fees', 'D. It increases the need for printers', 'A', 'Sharing resources like printers and storage reduces the need to buy multiples of the same device.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpret the meaning of this statement: ```A network is a group of interconnected people or things or a collection of computers connected through communication channels.```', 'A. Networks connect only computers, not people', 'B. Networks enable devices and users to exchange information via connections', 'C. Networks require physical cables only', 'D. Networks are collections of isolated devices', 'B', 'The statement emphasizes connectivity and communication between devices or users in a network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of these is an essential feature of a network?', 'A. Sharing data and resources among multiple devices', 'B. Each computer working independently without communication', 'C. Having the same hardware for all devices', 'D. Using only wireless technology', 'A', 'Resource and data sharing is fundamental to what defines a network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which option best explains what "communication channels" mean in networking?', 'A. The software protocols used for internet browsing', 'B. The physical or wireless links enabling data transfer between devices', 'C. The programming languages used for network applications', 'D. The power cables supplying electricity', 'B', 'Communication channels are the pathways (wired or wireless) through which data travels in a network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short Answer: Why is it important for a network to have at least one process on one device that can communicate with a process on another device?', 'A. To enable data exchange and resource sharing', 'B. To make devices physically close', 'C. To synchronize clocks', 'D. To run independent programs', 'A', 'Communication between processes is essential for network functionality such as data exchange and resource sharing.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Explain why a networked environment is preferred over stand-alone computers in large organizations. Consider resource management and collaboration.', 'A. Networks reduce costs, improve resource sharing, and enhance collaboration across users', 'B. Stand-alone computers are faster and easier to manage', 'C. Networks require more devices, increasing costs', 'D. Stand-alone systems provide better security', 'A', 'Networks enable efficient sharing of resources and information, supporting collaboration and reducing hardware costs.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Short Answer: Why is it important for a network to have at least one process on one device that can communicate with a process on another device?', 'A. To enable data exchange and resource sharing', 'B. To make devices physically close', 'C. To synchronize clocks', 'D. To run independent programs', 'A', 'Communication between processes is essential for network functionality such as data exchange and resource sharing.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Explain why a networked environment is preferred over stand-alone computers in large organizations. Consider resource management and collaboration.', 'A. Networks reduce costs, improve resource sharing, and enhance collaboration across users', 'B. Stand-alone computers are faster and easier to manage', 'C. Networks require more devices, increasing costs', 'D. Stand-alone systems provide better security', 'A', 'Networks enable efficient sharing of resources and information, supporting collaboration and reducing hardware costs.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'In a networked environment, describe the role of communication channels and why they are critical for maintaining network integrity and performance.', 'A. They act as pathways for data transmission and affect speed and reliability', 'B. They store files for users', 'C. They provide power to network devices', 'D. They encrypt user passwords', 'A', 'Communication channels are essential physical or wireless links that determine how data is transmitted, impacting the overall network''s performance and stability.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Interpretation: Given a scenario where two devices are connected but cannot communicate due to incompatible protocols, what does this imply about the requirements of network connectivity?', 'A. Devices must have compatible protocols to successfully communicate', 'B. Physical connection alone ensures communication', 'C. Devices always communicate regardless of protocol', 'D. Network size affects communication', 'A', 'Beyond physical connection, devices need compatible communication protocols to properly exchange data.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary advantage of sharing data over a network compared to using removable storage devices like floppy disks?', 'A. It is faster and more efficient', 'B. It is more expensive', 'C. It requires physical transportation', 'D. It limits user access', 'A', 'Networks enable fast data sharing without the need for physical movement of storage devices, improving efficiency.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A network allows central management of resources on multiple computers from a single location.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only in small networks', 'D. Only for printing devices', 'A', 'Networks provide centralized administration capabilities that improve resource management and control.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Before networks, sharing data across multiple computers primarily relied on ______.', 'A. removable storage devices like floppy disks', 'B. wireless communication', 'C. cloud services', 'D. email systems', 'A', 'Prior to networks, data sharing was done physically using removable media such as floppy disks.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'What binary code do computers use to communicate instructions?', 'A. A sequence of 0s and 1s', 'B. Hexadecimal numbers', 'C. Alphabet letters', 'D. Decimal digits', 'A', 'Computers communicate using binary code which consists of 0s and 1s to represent data and instructions.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Interpret the following scenario: ```As an IT administrator at a multinational bank with 5000 employees worldwide, how can a network help in managing software versions across all employee computers?```', 'A. The network allows remote verification and installation of software from a central computer', 'B. The administrator must visit each employee physically', 'C. The software must be manually installed via removable media', 'D. Software management is impossible without physical access', 'A', 'Networks enable centralized management, allowing IT to remotely check and update software on multiple devices globally.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why do organizations depend on networks to stay competitive?', 'A. Networks increase productivity by enabling resource sharing and centralized management', 'B. Networks are more expensive and complex', 'C. Networks limit communication among employees', 'D. Networks reduce data security', 'A', 'Networks improve operational efficiency through sharing and centralized control, supporting organizational competitiveness.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Data transferred across networks is represented internally as a sequence of ______.', 'A. 0s and 1s (binary)', 'B. ASCII characters', 'C. Hexadecimal codes', 'D. Decimal numbers', 'A', 'Network communication relies on binary sequences to encode and transmit data.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is one major limitation of using removable media like floppy disks compared to networks for data sharing?', 'A. Data transfer speed is much slower due to physical transport', 'B. It is more secure than networks', 'C. It allows multiple users to access files simultaneously', 'D. It is automated and centralized', 'A', 'Physical transportation of removable media significantly slows down data sharing compared to networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes how computers communicate over a network?', 'A. By sending and receiving binary data sequences (0s and 1s)', 'B. By speaking in human languages', 'C. By using hexadecimal codes only', 'D. By exchanging printed documents', 'A', 'Computers communicate digitally by sending binary data sequences representing instructions and information.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Managing software updates on thousands of computers manually without a network is efficient.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Sometimes', 'D. Only with special software', 'B', 'Manual updates on many machines are inefficient; networks enable remote and automated management.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Explain the concept of a "sneaker net" and its disadvantages compared to modern networking.', 'A. Data transfer via physical movement of storage devices; slower and less efficient than networks', 'B. Wireless data transfer method', 'C. Automated data backup system', 'D. Cloud-based file sharing', 'A', '"Sneaker net" refers to manually transferring data using physical media, which is slower and less convenient than networks.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Discuss how central management through networks can improve security in a multinational organization.', 'A. Allows consistent updates, monitoring, and enforcement of security policies across all devices', 'B. Requires individual management of each device', 'C. Reduces control over endpoints', 'D. Only manages hardware, not software', 'A', 'Centralized network management facilitates uniform security enforcement and timely updates across all connected devices.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Computers process and transmit data using ______ code, which consists of two symbols: 0 and 1.', 'A. binary', 'B. Morse', 'C. ASCII', 'D. hexadecimal', 'A', 'Binary code is the fundamental language of computers, using 0 and 1 for processing and communication.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: The speed and reliability of a network depend significantly on its communication channels.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only on software', 'D. Only on hardware specs', 'A', 'Communication channels (wired or wireless) directly affect how quickly and reliably data travels in a network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpret the meaning of this statement: ```Networks increase productivity by enabling resource sharing and centralized management.```', 'A. Networks allow multiple users to efficiently share hardware and software resources', 'B. Networks limit user access', 'C. Networks slow down computer performance', 'D. Networks are expensive and complicated', 'A', 'Sharing resources and centralized administration improve efficiency and productivity in organizations.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why is it important for computers in a network to use compatible communication protocols?', 'A. To ensure successful data exchange and understanding between devices', 'B. To make hardware cheaper', 'C. To reduce electricity consumption', 'D. To prevent internet access', 'A', 'Protocols standardize data exchange ensuring devices can communicate properly and interpret messages correctly.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Short Answer: Describe how binary code represents data inside a computer network.', 'A. Data is encoded as sequences of 0s and 1s representing instructions and information', 'B. Data is represented by text files only', 'C. Data is only in hexadecimal', 'D. Data is stored as images', 'A', 'Binary sequences form the fundamental way computers encode and communicate data.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'What is a network "resource"?', 'A. Hardware or data shared over a network such as printers, files, and applications', 'B. Only the internet connection', 'C. A single computer''s local storage', 'D. Power supply units', 'A', 'Resources are shared devices and data accessible by multiple users on a network.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Networks can only connect computers within the same physical building.', 'A. False', 'B. True', 'C. Only with special cables', 'D. Only in small companies', 'A', 'Networks can span local, wide, or global areas, connecting devices across buildings and even countries.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best explains why organizations use networks instead of stand-alone computers?', 'A. Networks enable sharing resources, improve collaboration, and allow centralized management', 'B. Stand-alone computers are cheaper to maintain', 'C. Stand-alone computers provide better network security', 'D. Networks reduce computer speed', 'A', 'Networks increase efficiency by allowing users to share resources and manage devices centrally.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which of the following best describes the role of network protocols?', 'A. Define rules and standards for data communication between devices', 'B. Increase the physical speed of data cables', 'C. Manage electrical power to devices', 'D. Store files on a network server', 'A', 'Network protocols ensure devices communicate effectively by following agreed-upon rules for data exchange.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: In a networked system, a ______ acts as an intermediary device that directs data packets to their destination.', 'A. router', 'B. printer', 'C. hard drive', 'D. monitor', 'A', 'Routers are network devices that route data packets between different networks or devices.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A network administrator can remotely install software updates on all connected devices through the network.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only on wireless networks', 'D. Only with physical access', 'A', 'Network administration tools allow remote management including software updates on multiple devices.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)?', 'A. A network connecting computers within a single building', 'B. A group of connected LANs in the same geographical area, also called a Campus Area Network (CAN)', 'C. The largest network spanning the entire globe', 'D. A small network connecting personal devices', 'B', 'A MAN connects multiple LANs within a city or campus area and is sometimes called a Campus Area Network (CAN).', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A Wide Area Network (WAN) connects LANs over a large geographical area, such as countries or continents.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only for small buildings', 'D. Only wireless networks', 'A', 'WANs cover wide geographic areas and interconnect multiple LANs, like the Internet.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: The smallest type of network connecting personal devices like phones and wearables is called a ______.', 'A. Personal Area Network (PAN)', 'B. Local Area Network (LAN)', 'C. Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)', 'D. Wide Area Network (WAN)', 'A', 'A PAN connects personal devices within a very limited area, such as around a single person.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Which statement best describes a peer-to-peer network?', 'A. All computers have equal authority and communicate directly', 'B. One central server controls all resources', 'C. It only connects personal devices', 'D. It is the largest type of network', 'A', 'In peer-to-peer networks, each computer can share resources equally without a central authority.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpretation: In a peer-to-peer network, can a single computer restrict access to some of its shared resources while allowing others?', 'A. Yes, each computer can configure resource sharing independently', 'B. No, all resources are shared equally', 'C. Only the administrator can restrict access', 'D. Resource sharing is always public', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks allow each computer to control which resources it shares or restricts.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: The Internet is the largest example of a ______ network.', 'A. Wide Area Network (WAN)', 'B. Local Area Network (LAN)', 'C. Personal Area Network (PAN)', 'D. Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)', 'A', 'The Internet spans worldwide, making it the largest WAN.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which type of network typically uses different transmission methods and media compared to LANs?', 'A. MANs and WANs', 'B. PANs', 'C. Peer-to-peer networks', 'D. Client/server networks', 'A', 'MANs and WANs often use diverse transmission technologies unlike the more uniform LANs.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Explain why peer-to-peer networks are considered simpler compared to client/server networks.', 'A. Because all computers have equal authority and no dedicated server is needed', 'B. Because they connect fewer devices', 'C. Because they only use wireless connections', 'D. Because they are managed by a central authority', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks lack centralized control, making them simpler but less scalable.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Describe a major limitation of peer-to-peer networks in large organizations.', 'A. Lack of centralized control can cause security and management challenges', 'B. They are too expensive to implement', 'C. They require complex hardware', 'D. They cannot share files', 'A', 'Without central control, peer-to-peer networks can be harder to secure and manage in large-scale environments.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of a Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)?', 'A. It connects LANs within a city or campus', 'B. It covers a wide geographical area such as multiple cities', 'C. It is also known as a Campus Area Network (CAN)', 'D. It is larger than a LAN but smaller than a WAN', 'B', 'MANs typically cover a city or campus, not multiple cities (which would be WAN territory).', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: The transmission methods and media used by MANs and WANs are always the same as those used by LANs.', 'A. False', 'B. True', 'C. Only in wireless networks', 'D. Only when networks are small', 'A', 'MANs and WANs often employ different transmission methods and media than LANs due to their larger scale.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: In a peer-to-peer network, every computer can ______ with every other computer directly.', 'A. communicate', 'B. control', 'C. limit', 'D. disconnect', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks allow direct communication between all computers without intermediaries.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpret the following: ```A company uses a MAN to connect multiple LANs across its office buildings in the city. What is the primary benefit of this setup?```', 'A. Efficient sharing of resources across a large geographical area within the city', 'B. Complete isolation of each LAN', 'C. Reduced communication speed', 'D. Only personal device connection', 'A', 'A MAN enables connected LANs to share data and resources efficiently over a metropolitan area.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Personal Area Networks (PANs) are typically used to connect devices like smartphones and wearable tech around a person.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only in offices', 'D. Only in data centers', 'A', 'PANs cover a very small area such as around a single person and connect personal devices.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'What differentiates a WAN from a LAN?', 'A. WAN covers a wide geographical area while LAN covers a small localized area', 'B. WAN is only wireless, LAN is wired', 'C. LAN is always faster than WAN', 'D. WAN only connects personal devices', 'A', 'WANs span large geographic regions; LANs are confined to smaller areas like offices or homes.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: A Campus Area Network (CAN) is another name for a ______.', 'A. Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)', 'B. Wide Area Network (WAN)', 'C. Personal Area Network (PAN)', 'D. Local Area Network (LAN)', 'A', 'CAN is synonymous with MAN, referring to connected LANs within a specific campus or city area.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which network model typically involves a central server managing resource access?', 'A. Client/server network', 'B. Peer-to-peer network', 'C. Personal Area Network', 'D. Metropolitan Area Network', 'A', 'Client/server networks have a central server that controls resource sharing and access.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Explain why MANs and WANs often require different transmission media than LANs.', 'A. Because they cover larger areas and require technologies suited for long distances', 'B. Because LANs do not use cables', 'C. Because MANs and WANs only connect personal devices', 'D. Because LANs are wireless only', 'A', 'Longer distances in MANs and WANs necessitate more robust transmission methods and media than typical LANs.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which network type is best suited for connecting devices within a single office building?', 'A. Local Area Network (LAN)', 'B. Wide Area Network (WAN)', 'C. Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)', 'D. Personal Area Network (PAN)', 'A', 'LANs are designed to cover small, localized areas such as a single building or office.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: In a client/server network, the ______ controls access to shared resources and services.', 'A. server', 'B. client', 'C. peer', 'D. router', 'A', 'The server manages and controls access to resources in a client/server architecture.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Peer-to-peer networks are typically more scalable and secure than client/server networks.', 'A. False', 'B. True', 'C. Only when encrypted', 'D. Only with specialized hardware', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks lack centralized control, making them less scalable and less secure than client/server networks.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'What is a key advantage of traditional peer-to-peer networks?', 'A. Simple to configure with low technical expertise needed', 'B. Highly secure and centrally managed', 'C. Scalable to thousands of users easily', 'D. Always expensive to maintain', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks are easy to set up and require minimal technical knowledge.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Peer-to-peer networks are ideal for large organizations with many users.', 'A. False', 'B. True', 'C. Only if secured', 'D. Only with dedicated servers', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks do not scale well and are not suitable for large numbers of users.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: In a peer-to-peer network, resource sharing is managed by ______.', 'A. each individual user', 'B. a central server', 'C. network administrator', 'D. cloud services', 'A', 'Each user controls what resources they share, with no central authority.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Peer-to-peer networks rely on a central computer or authority for managing shared resources.', 'A. False', 'B. True', 'C. Only for files', 'D. Only for printers', 'A', 'Each user controls their own resources; there is no central management.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which environment is best suited for a peer-to-peer network?', 'A. Small office with limited users and minimal technical expertise', 'B. Large enterprise with thousands of users', 'C. Cloud data centers', 'D. Public internet', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks are simple, cost-effective, and suitable for small groups.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: The smallest type of network connecting personal devices like phones and wearables is called a ______.', 'A. Personal Area Network (PAN)', 'B. Local Area Network (LAN)', 'C. Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)', 'D. Wide Area Network (WAN)', 'A', 'A PAN connects personal devices within a very limited area, such as around a single person.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'What is a Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)?', 'A. A network connecting computers within a single building', 'B. A group of connected LANs in the same geographical area, also called a Campus Area Network (CAN)', 'C. The largest network spanning the entire globe', 'D. A small network connecting personal devices', 'B', 'A MAN connects multiple LANs within a city or campus area and is sometimes called a Campus Area Network (CAN).', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'What is a disadvantage of peer-to-peer networks when many computers are connected?', 'A. Difficulty managing and tracking shared resources', 'B. Increased central control', 'C. High setup cost', 'D. Too much security', 'A', 'More computers make it harder to manage files and versions without a central system.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: A Campus Area Network (CAN) is another name for a ______.', 'A. Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)', 'B. Wide Area Network (WAN)', 'C. Personal Area Network (PAN)', 'D. Local Area Network (LAN)', 'A', 'CAN is synonymous with MAN, referring to connected LANs within a specific campus or city area.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpret this scenario:
```User A creates a directory ''SharedDocs'' on her computer and allows all networked computers to read its files. What does this illustrate?```', 'A. User-configured file sharing on a peer-to-peer network', 'B. Centralized resource management', 'C. Cloud-based storage', 'D. Server controlled sharing', 'A', 'In peer-to-peer networks, users individually configure file sharing.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Peer-to-peer networks always ensure uniform and secure access to shared resources.', 'A. False', 'B. True', 'C. Only with encryption', 'D. Only on small networks', 'A', 'Access is managed individually, so security and uniformity vary widely.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which type of network typically uses different transmission methods and media compared to LANs?', 'A. MANs and WANs', 'B. PANs', 'C. Peer-to-peer networks', 'D. Client/server networks', 'A', 'MANs and WANs often use diverse transmission technologies unlike the more uniform LANs.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why are peer-to-peer networks generally less expensive than client/server networks?', 'A. They require no dedicated servers or specialized hardware', 'B. They use expensive cloud services', 'C. They need professional network administrators', 'D. They rely on external service providers', 'A', 'No dedicated infrastructure reduces setup and maintenance costs.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: In a peer-to-peer network, every computer can ______ with every other computer directly.', 'A. communicate', 'B. control', 'C. limit', 'D. disconnect', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks allow direct communication between all computers without intermediaries.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Explain why peer-to-peer networks might pose security risks.', 'A. Because resource access is controlled individually and may not be uniformly secured', 'B. Because they use advanced encryption', 'C. Because they only connect personal devices', 'D. Because they require expensive hardware', 'A', 'Inconsistent user configurations can leave resources vulnerable to unauthorized access.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: What happens if multiple users save different versions of the same file on their own hard drives in a peer-to-peer network?', 'A. It becomes difficult to track the most current version', 'B. The network merges the files automatically', 'C. The server controls which version is valid', 'D. Versions are always synchronized', 'A', 'Peer-to-peer networks lack centralized version control, causing confusion.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Why are peer-to-peer networks less flexible as they grow larger?', 'A. Resource management becomes complex and lacks central control', 'B. More users increase bandwidth automatically', 'C. They become more secure', 'D. They require dedicated servers', 'A', 'Without central administration, managing many shared resources becomes difficult.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of a Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)?', 'A. It connects LANs within a city or campus', 'B. It covers a wide geographical area such as multiple cities', 'C. It is also known as a Campus Area Network (CAN)', 'D. It is larger than a LAN but smaller than a WAN', 'B', 'MANs typically cover a city or campus, not multiple cities (which would be WAN territory).', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'Explain why MANs and WANs often require different transmission media than LANs.', 'A. Because they cover larger areas and require technologies suited for long distances', 'B. Because LANs do not use cables', 'C. Because MANs and WANs only connect personal devices', 'D. Because LANs are wireless only', 'A', 'Longer distances in MANs and WANs necessitate more robust transmission methods and media than typical LANs.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which network model typically involves a central server managing resource access?', 'A. Client/server network', 'B. Peer-to-peer network', 'C. Personal Area Network', 'D. Metropolitan Area Network', 'A', 'Client/server networks have a central server that controls resource sharing and access.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: In a client/server network, what role does the server play?', 'A. It manages and controls access to shared resources', 'B. It only sends emails', 'C. It connects personal devices only', 'D. It acts as a backup user', 'A', 'Servers in client/server networks control resource sharing and user access centrally.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A Wide Area Network (WAN) can connect devices across different countries.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only with satellite connections', 'D. Only within the same continent', 'A', 'WANs span large geographic areas, including multiple countries and continents.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpretation: 
```A user notices that when they save a document on their computer in a peer-to-peer network, colleagues cannot immediately see the updated version. Why might this occur?```', 'A. Because each user manages their own files independently without centralized syncing', 'B. Because the server has blocked the update', 'C. Because the network automatically merges versions', 'D. Because files are stored only on cloud servers', 'A', 'In peer-to-peer networks, lack of central control means file versions may not synchronize automatically.', 'hard');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: What distinguishes modern P2P networks like BitTorrent from traditional peer-to-peer networks?', 'A. They require specialized software beyond the OS', 'B. They do not share files between computers', 'C. They rely on a central server to manage files', 'D. They operate only within local area networks', 'A', 'Modern P2P networks use specialized software to manage resource sharing over the Internet.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Napster was forced to shut down in 2001 because of security vulnerabilities in its software.', 'A. False', 'B. True', 'C. Partially true', 'D. Not applicable', 'A', 'Napster was shut down due to copyright infringement lawsuits, not software security issues.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: In a client/server network, the central computer facilitating communication is called the ______.', 'A. server', 'B. client', 'C. peer', 'D. router', 'A', 'The server manages resource sharing and communication in client/server networks.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Interpretation: 
