VALUES (4, 'MCQ: What best describes a client in a client/server network?', 'A. A workstation that requests resources from the server', 'B. The central computer that manages resources', 'C. A peer computer that manages its own resources', 'D. A network device that routes traffic', 'A', 'Clients are computers that rely on servers to provide resources and services.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which of the following is NOT an example of a modern P2P network?', 'A. Gnutella', 'B. Freenet', 'C. Napster (original)', 'D. HTTP web server', 'D', 'HTTP web servers operate on client/server architecture, not peer-to-peer.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Client/server networks can be compared to a public library where a librarian controls access to resources.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only if library is digital', 'D. Only in small networks', 'A', 'In client/server networks, servers control resource access much like a librarian controls library resources.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Which company pioneered the high-speed data transfer technology known as BitTorrent?', 'A. BitTorrent, Inc.', 'B. Napster', 'C. Gnutella', 'D. Freenet', 'A', 'BitTorrent, Inc. developed the BitTorrent protocol used for fast distributed file sharing.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Traditional peer-to-peer networks typically operate within a ______ or small office environment.', 'A. home', 'B. data center', 'C. multinational company', 'D. cloud environment', 'A', 'They are usually small and localized, suitable for homes or small offices.', 'easy');
VALUES (4, 'Short answer: What was the primary legal issue that forced Napster to cease its original file-sharing operations in 2001?', 'A. Copyright infringement', 'B. Network security flaws', 'C. Hardware failures', 'D. Lack of user interest', 'A', 'Napster was sued for enabling unauthorized sharing of copyrighted music.', 'medium');
