VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes how device servers can handle serial data from cameras?', 'A. Convert serial data to multiple IP streams', 'B. Convert IP data to serial protocols', 'C. Block serial data to reduce network load', 'D. Store data only without transmission', 'A', 'Device servers can map serial device data to multiple IP addresses for flexible usage.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why is configuring a device server to send data only when available beneficial?', 'A. Saves bandwidth and reduces network traffic', 'B. Ensures continuous data transmission', 'C. Increases power consumption', 'D. Prevents data from being recorded', 'A', 'Transmitting only when data is available optimizes network resource usage.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: How can device servers assist in ensuring security compliance with municipalities?', 'A. By providing flexible video data transmission options', 'B. By disabling cameras remotely', 'C. By encrypting all video data', 'D. By requiring physical access to data', 'A', 'Device servers support multiple transmission modes, helping meet local proof requirements.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Device servers cannot send data to multiple IP addresses simultaneously.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Device servers can multicast data to multiple IP addresses for recording and archival.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which is NOT a configuration option for device servers in handling camera data?', 'A. Open continuous data pipe', 'B. Send data only when buffer has data', 'C. Transmit data to multiple IP addresses', 'D. Automatically disable camera when motion detected', 'D', 'Disabling cameras on motion is not a typical device server feature; the focus is on data transmission.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Device server technology provides the user with many options for tuning the device to meet the ________ needs of their application.', 'A. general', 'B. specific', 'C. minimal', 'D. default', 'B', 'Device servers offer configurable options tailored to the unique needs of different applications.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes how device servers are used with bar code readers in a warehouse?', 'A. They process inventory locally on handheld devices', 'B. They link remote bar code readers to the network for real-time inventory updates', 'C. They replace bar code readers with manual input', 'D. They only store scanned data locally without network connectivity', 'B', 'Device servers connect remote scanners to the network allowing instant inventory tracking.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Device server technology can be applied to point-of-sale debit card scanners to enable automatic employee debit tracking.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Device servers network-enable debit card scanners for automatic transaction processing.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a benefit of using device server technology in medical applications?', 'A. Increased need for dedicated PCs at each medical device', 'B. Linking various devices into one seamless network for efficient service', 'C. Limiting physician access to patient data', 'D. Replacing medical devices with manual paper-based methods', 'B', 'Device servers integrate multiple medical devices into a network improving data accessibility.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Device servers help medical labs by replacing _______ at each device, lowering equipment costs.', 'A. internet connections', 'B. dedicated PCs', 'C. manual logs', 'D. printers', 'B', 'Device servers allow network connectivity without requiring a dedicated PC per device.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which industry example shows device servers helping prevent ticket theft and resale?', 'A. Hospitals', 'B. Amusement parks', 'C. Warehouses', 'D. Factories', 'B', 'Amusement parks use device servers with debit card scanners to deter theft and resale.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: How do device servers improve efficiency in medical facilities?', 'A. By enabling network connectivity for diagnostic devices', 'B. By requiring more PCs', 'C. By disconnecting devices from networks', 'D. By increasing manual data entry', 'A', 'Networked devices allow faster, centralized data access and improved workflow.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Device server technology can only be applied to new medical devices with modern communication protocols.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Device servers support legacy serial communication, enabling integration of older devices.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes the role of device servers in remote bar code scanning?', 'A. Converting serial input to network data for real-time processing', 'B. Storing scanned data without network transmission', 'C. Encrypting all scanned data manually', 'D. Replacing the bar code scanners entirely', 'A', 'Device servers translate serial scanner data into network packets for immediate use.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Device server technology allows physicians immediate access to patient data over the ________.', 'A. Internet', 'B. Local PC', 'C. Paper files', 'D. Manual logs', 'A', 'Internet connectivity enables remote access to medical device data and patient information.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT a benefit of device servers in scanning or medical applications?', 'A. Improved data sharing and efficiency', 'B. Lower equipment costs by reducing dedicated PCs', 'C. Enabling remote monitoring and control', 'D. Complete replacement of all existing hardware', 'D', 'Device servers integrate and network-enable existing hardware rather than replace it.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes a device server?', 'A. A PC with special software for serial communication', 'B. A specialized network-based hardware device that functions independently of OS or proprietary protocols', 'C. A unit used only to connect printers on a network', 'D. A modem used to access the internet', 'B', 'Device servers are dedicated hardware that manage serial devices on networks without dependence on operating systems or proprietary protocols.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Device servers are typically more expensive than PCs when considering hardware and software development costs.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Device servers cost a fraction of PCs, especially when factoring in reduced software development and man-hours.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What software allows a networked PC to connect to a device server and use the original serial application without modification?', 'A. Port redirector software', 'B. Antivirus software', 'C. Device driver installer', 'D. Network firewall', 'A', 'Port redirector software maps network ports to serial ports, enabling legacy applications to communicate over the network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: A terminal server traditionally connects asynchronous devices such as _______, printers, hosts, and modems to a LAN or WAN.', 'A. routers', 'B. terminals', 'C. switches', 'D. PCs', 'B', 'Terminal servers specifically connect asynchronous terminals and related devices to networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which term describes software that lets a user access multiple serial device consoles from anywhere on a network?', 'A. Console manager', 'B. Device server', 'C. Console server', 'D. Print server', 'C', 'Console server software centralizes access to serial consoles remotely.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Print servers are hardware devices designed to connect and manage shared printers over a network.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Print servers provide centralized printer management on networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a major benefit of using device servers in medical facilities compared to having a dedicated PC at each device?', 'A. Higher hardware costs', 'B. Reduced need for software development on each PC', 'C. Increased manual data entry', 'D. More complex system architecture', 'B', 'Device servers allow legacy applications to run on networked PCs with minimal modification, reducing development.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best defines a console manager?', 'A. A device to print documents over a network', 'B. Software or hardware that allows remote management of serial devices including routers and switches', 'C. A modem connecting to the internet', 'D. A PC used to monitor network traffic', 'B', 'Console managers enable remote control and monitoring of network equipment through serial connections.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: Why is device server technology considered flexible across many markets?', 'A. Because it requires proprietary operating systems', 'B. Because it only supports printers', 'C. Because it allows legacy and new devices to connect to networks easily', 'D. Because it needs custom hardware for every application', 'C', 'Device servers provide network connectivity for various devices, supporting many markets with minimal changes.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: The cost calculation of device servers includes not only hardware but also _______ saved by reducing software development time.', 'A. electricity', 'B. man-hours', 'C. internet bandwidth', 'D. physical space', 'B', 'Device servers save man-hours required to rewrite or adapt software for network communication.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes the primary function of a network switch?', 'A. It broadcasts all incoming packets to every port', 'B. It examines packet addresses and forwards packets only to the correct segment', 'C. It acts as a firewall to block unauthorized access', 'D. It stores data for backup purposes', 'B', 'Unlike hubs, switches analyze the source and destination addresses of packets and forward them only where needed, improving efficiency.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A switch regenerates forwarded packets, allowing for longer network distances and more connected nodes.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Packet regeneration by switches enables greater distances between devices and supports larger networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What happens when a switch receives a packet where the source and destination are on the same network segment?', 'A. The packet is forwarded to another segment', 'B. The packet is dropped or filtered', 'C. The packet is duplicated', 'D. The switch broadcasts the packet to all ports', 'B', 'Switches filter packets when source and destination are on the same segment to reduce unnecessary traffic.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Each segment in a switched network is an independent ______ domain.', 'A. broadcast', 'B. collision', 'C. routing', 'D. security', 'B', 'Switching creates separate collision domains, reducing collisions and improving network performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which statement correctly contrasts switches with hubs?', 'A. Hubs filter packets based on MAC addresses, switches do not', 'B. Switches forward packets only to intended ports, hubs broadcast to all ports', 'C. Switches operate at the physical layer, hubs operate at the network layer', 'D. Hubs create separate collision domains, switches do not', 'B', 'Hubs broadcast incoming packets to all ports, while switches selectively forward packets.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Using switches in a network allows multiple computers to send data simultaneously, increasing throughput.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Switches enable parallel transmissions by splitting collision domains, allowing multiple simultaneous communications.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: What is the main benefit of filtering bad or misaligned packets in a switched network?', 'A. Increases data redundancy', 'B. Prevents network congestion and errors', 'C. Speeds up packet duplication', 'D. Reduces network security', 'B', 'Filtering reduces the spread of corrupted packets, enhancing network reliability.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: The ability of switches to split a network into separate collision domains helps to ______ overall network collision rates.', 'A. increase', 'B. maintain', 'C. dramatically lower', 'D. ignore', 'C', 'Each collision domain is isolated, so collisions are confined and overall collision rates drop.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'MCQ: Before adding switches to your network design, what is an important consideration?', 'A. Whether the network uses fiber optic cables only', 'B. If switching will benefit the network’s speed and capacity', 'C. The color of the switch hardware', 'D. If all devices are from the same manufacturer', 'B', 'Evaluating the network’s needs helps ensure switches provide actual performance improvements.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpretation: Explain why switches can improve network capacity compared to hubs.', 'A. Because switches reduce collisions by creating multiple collision domains and forward traffic only to intended recipients', 'B. Because switches increase the number of cables used', 'C. Because switches have larger physical sizes', 'D. Because switches encrypt all network traffic', 'A', 'By isolating collision domains and filtering traffic, switches allow more efficient data transfer and higher network capacity.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What feature makes switches an attractive alternative to hubs?', 'A. Complex installation process', 'B. Manual configuration of Ethernet addresses', 'C. Self-learning and plug-and-play functionality', 'D. Use of fiber optic cables only', 'C', 'Switches automatically build a table of Ethernet addresses, requiring little setup, making them easy to use.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a ''collapsed backbone'' network?', 'A. A network that uses only hubs for connectivity', 'B. A network that uses switches connected by high-speed uplinks', 'C. A network without any backbone cabling', 'D. A network with limited user capacity', 'B', 'High-speed links between switches form a centralized, efficient core called a collapsed backbone.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why might a network dedicate switch ports to individual nodes?', 'A. To reduce electricity usage', 'B. To provide full bandwidth to high-traffic computers', 'C. To minimize software licensing', 'D. To avoid using Ethernet protocol', 'B', 'Dedicated ports isolate traffic, giving critical nodes full access to bandwidth for better performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is required for full duplex communication?', 'A. Only the switch must support full duplex', 'B. Both the NIC and switch must support full duplex', 'C. Only the NIC must support full duplex', 'D. A router must be present', 'B', 'Full duplex requires both ends of the link to support it for simultaneous two-way communication.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Full duplex doubles the potential bandwidth on a link.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Full duplex allows sending and receiving simultaneously, effectively doubling the link’s bandwidth.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What happens as more users and applications are added to a shared network?', 'A. Performance improves', 'B. Collisions decrease', 'C. Network performance deteriorates', 'D. Network automatically balances load', 'C', 'More users lead to higher competition for the Ethernet bus, causing congestion and performance drops.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is a typical throughput of a moderately loaded 10 Mbps Ethernet network?', 'A. 9 Mbps', 'B. 2.5 Mbps', 'C. 7 Mbps', 'D. 5 Mbps', 'B', 'After accounting for overhead and collisions, usable throughput drops to around 2.5 Mbps on a 10 Mbps network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which Ethernet type is likely to experience more collisions with added nodes?', 'A. Full duplex Ethernet', 'B. Shared Ethernet', 'C. Point-to-point Ethernet', 'D. Token ring', 'B', 'Shared Ethernet uses a single collision domain, so more nodes increase the chance of collisions.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: A network where each user competes for the Ethernet bus is called a _______ network.', 'A. switched', 'B. full duplex', 'C. shared', 'D. token ring', 'C', 'In a shared network, all nodes compete for access to the transmission medium (Ethernet bus).', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpret: Why is Fast Ethernet more suitable than 10 Mbps Ethernet for networks with high data demands?', 'A. It operates over fiber only', 'B. It offers higher raw and usable bandwidth', 'C. It reduces software dependencies', 'D. It simplifies switch configuration', 'B', 'Fast Ethernet provides more capacity, making it more effective for handling increased traffic without congestion.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why are switches considered easier to install and manage compared to hubs?', 'A. They require manual MAC address configuration', 'B. They are plug-and-play and self-learning', 'C. They only support old Ethernet standards', 'D. They don''t support uplink capabilities', 'B', 'Switches automatically learn the MAC addresses of connected devices and build forwarding tables, eliminating the need for manual setup.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes a ''collapsed backbone'' network?', 'A. A series of daisy-chained hubs', 'B. A network using wireless mesh connections', 'C. A network of switches linked via high-speed uplinks', 'D. A network with a single server connected to multiple clients', 'C', 'A collapsed backbone network uses high-speed connections between switches to form a central structure, improving speed and efficiency.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Full duplex communication on a switch port doubles the potential bandwidth for that link.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Full duplex allows simultaneous send and receive operations, effectively doubling the link''s bandwidth.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which users should ideally be connected to a dedicated switch port for optimal network performance?', 'A. Occasional internet browsers', 'B. Mobile users with weak signal', 'C. Servers and power users', 'D. Guest network users', 'C', 'Critical computers with high traffic benefit from dedicated switch ports, which reduce contention and collisions.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'In Ethernet networking, what is the main cause of reduced performance as more users are added?', 'A. Increased wireless interference', 'B. High memory usage on switches', 'C. Competition for the Ethernet bus', 'D. Overloaded DNS servers', 'C', 'In shared Ethernet environments, all nodes share the medium, leading to collisions and lower throughput as user count increases.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the approximate throughput of a moderately loaded 10 Mbps Ethernet network?', 'A. 10 Mbps', 'B. 5 Mbps', 'C. 2.5 Mbps', 'D. 1 Mbps', 'C', 'Due to packet overhead, inter-packet gaps, and collisions, effective throughput is typically around 2.5 Mbps on a 10 Mbps Ethernet.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following improves bandwidth for critical servers in a switched network?', 'A. Using hubs instead of switches', 'B. Connecting multiple servers to a single port', 'C. Assigning a dedicated port with full duplex support', 'D. Turning off full duplex mode', 'C', 'This setup reduces collisions and doubles potential bandwidth, improving server communication.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Fast Ethernet links between switches can be used to ______.', 'A. Increase collisions', 'B. Reduce transmission speed', 'C. Provide high-speed uplinks', 'D. Disable MAC learning', 'C', 'Fast Ethernet links are commonly used to connect switches in backbone networks for higher-speed data exchange.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which network type experiences increased collisions as more traffic or nodes are added?', 'A. Fully switched Ethernet', 'B. Shared Ethernet', 'C. Wireless LANs with MIMO', 'D. Token ring networks', 'B', 'Shared Ethernet operates in a collision domain where multiple devices contend for the same bandwidth, increasing collisions with load.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: Describe the purpose of a switch''s MAC address table in one sentence.', 'A. It logs internet activity', 'B. It stores IP addresses for routing', 'C. It maps Ethernet addresses to ports for packet forwarding', 'D. It stores DNS entries', 'C', 'The MAC table enables switches to direct packets only to the appropriate port, improving network efficiency and reducing congestion.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does network utilization rate measure?', 'A. Number of devices connected to a network', 'B. Percentage of time the network is actively transmitting data', 'C. Total amount of installed cabling', 'D. Speed of the switch processor', 'B', 'Utilization rate reflects how much of the network''s capacity is being used during a given time period.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'At what average utilization rate do most networks begin to experience performance issues?', 'A. 15%', 'B. 25%', 'C. 35%', 'D. 50%', 'C', 'Utilization above 35% is considered near the optimum threshold where performance issues may start occurring.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A switch working at ''wire speed'' processes Ethernet frames faster than the network traffic allows.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Most modern switches can handle packets at full Ethernet speeds, even at minimum packet size, which is referred to as ''wire speed''.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is one reason routers introduce more latency compared to switches?', 'A. Routers use slower cables', 'B. Routers recalculate checksums and rewrite MAC headers', 'C. Routers are only used in wireless networks', 'D. Routers avoid filtering network traffic', 'B', 'These additional processing tasks in routers cause more delay (latency) compared to simpler devices like switches.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the maximum wire speed for Fast Ethernet in packets per second (pps)?', 'A. 14,480 pps', 'B. 100,000 pps', 'C. 148,800 pps', 'D. 1,000,000 pps', 'C', 'Fast Ethernet is capable of processing up to 148,800 packets per second when operating at wire speed.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following devices divides a network logically rather than physically?', 'A. Switch', 'B. Hub', 'C. Router', 'D. Repeater', 'C', 'Routers segment networks based on IP subnets, enabling logical division rather than physical.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Routers filter traffic based on _______.', 'A. port numbers', 'B. packet addresses', 'C. specific protocol', 'D. MAC addresses', 'C', 'Routers analyze protocol information to determine whether to forward or filter packets.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What key benefit do routers provide in terms of broadcast traffic?', 'A. They amplify broadcast signals', 'B. They automatically forward broadcasts to all segments', 'C. They filter out unnecessary broadcast traffic', 'D. They combine all broadcasts into one segment', 'C', 'Routers help reduce network congestion by blocking broadcast traffic that doesn''t need to cross segments.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which device is more efficient for simple packet forwarding: a switch or a router?', 'A. Router', 'B. Switch', 'C. Bridge', 'D. Hub', 'B', 'Switches only inspect MAC addresses and are optimized for faster forwarding with lower latency.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: What performance impact can high peak load deviation have on a network?', 'A. Causes utilization rate to drop suddenly', 'B. Increases latency on wireless channels', 'C. Leads to inconsistent traffic patterns affecting efficiency', 'D. Reduces the number of usable IP addresses', 'C', 'Large variations between average and peak traffic can signal inefficiencies or cause congestion issues.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is one major benefit of using a network switch instead of a hub?', 'A. Switches are cheaper than hubs', 'B. Switches require advanced configuration', 'C. Switches isolate traffic and reduce network congestion', 'D. Switches increase the number of collisions', 'C', 'Switches segment networks and isolate traffic, leading to reduced collisions and less congestion.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Switches create a single collision domain across all ports.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Each port on a switch is its own collision domain, unlike hubs which share one collision domain across all ports.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why are switches easier to deploy than expected despite their advanced features?', 'A. They come with pre-installed software', 'B. They operate at Layer 7 of the OSI model', 'C. They are self-learning and require no complex setup', 'D. They automatically upgrade hubs', 'C', 'Switches are self-learning and function on the same layer as hubs, making them easy to plug in and use.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a drawback of using switches in terms of packet handling?', 'A. They discard packets frequently', 'B. They cause higher bandwidth usage', 'C. They introduce latency in packet processing', 'D. They compress packets, reducing quality', 'C', 'Switches add small delays due to packet processing, which can accumulate in unnecessary deployments.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is a benefit of switch-based network segmentation?', 'A. Increases the number of repeaters needed', 'B. Resets distance and repeater limitations', 'C. Reduces the number of devices needed', 'D. Eliminates the need for IP addresses', 'B', 'Switch segmentation allows the restarting of distance and repeater rules for each network segment.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Monitoring a network becomes simpler when switches are used.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Switches make network monitoring more complicated due to segmentation and multiple domains.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the trend in switch vs. hub pricing mentioned in the content?', 'A. Switch prices are increasing rapidly', 'B. Hub prices are increasing faster than switch prices', 'C. Switch prices are dropping while hub prices remain stable', 'D. Both hub and switch prices are increasing equally', 'C', 'Switch prices are falling rapidly, narrowing the cost gap between them and the more mature hub technology.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a potential risk of deploying switches unnecessarily?', 'A. Increased collisions', 'B. Slower network performance due to added latency', 'C. Protocol mismatches', 'D. Loss of IP addresses', 'B', 'Unnecessary switches can increase packet latency, which may reduce overall network performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: The use of switches can ______ the impact of traffic congestion on a network.', 'A. worsen', 'B. ignore', 'C. relieve', 'D. duplicate', 'C', 'Switches isolate traffic into segments, helping reduce and relieve traffic congestion.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What makes identifying the performance impact of switches on congested networks difficult?', 'A. The congestion happens at the application level', 'B. Switches cause unpredictable traffic patterns', 'C. The performance gain depends on the degree of congestion', 'D. The switching technology is not yet standardized', 'C', 'While switches reduce congestion, the measurable improvement depends on how congested the network is, making it harder to quantify upfront.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the main goal of using a switch in a network?', 'A. To increase packet collisions', 'B. To eliminate or filter as much traffic as possible', 'C. To replace routers', 'D. To reduce network bandwidth', 'B', 'Switches are most beneficial when they reduce unnecessary traffic by filtering packets instead of forwarding all of them.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Adding a switch to a network that is not congested always improves performance.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Adding a switch to a non-congested network may introduce delays and retransmissions, possibly degrading performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is a symptom of network congestion?', 'A. Low utilization rates', 'B. Decreased collision rates', 'C. High packet retransmissions', 'D. Minimal packet delays', 'C', 'Network congestion leads to increased collisions and retransmissions, which reduce performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'At what utilization percentage is a network considered a good candidate for switching improvements?', 'A. Less than 10%', 'B. More than 35%', 'C. Between 5% and 15%', 'D. Exactly 50%', 'B', 'A network with utilization over 35% may benefit significantly from switching to reduce traffic bottlenecks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the typical maximum bandwidth for a Fast Ethernet network?', 'A. 10 Mbps', 'B. 50 Mbps', 'C. 100 Mbps', 'D. 1 Gbps', 'C', 'Fast Ethernet supports a theoretical maximum speed of 100 Mbps.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which condition indicates a performance issue in a network?', 'A. Utilization under 10%', 'B. Collision rate above 10%', 'C. Low user traffic', 'D. Buffer overflows under 1%', 'B', 'A high collision rate (over 10%) indicates that many packets are colliding and need retransmission, degrading performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What do increased Ethernet collisions typically lead to?', 'A. Faster throughput', 'B. Reduced retransmissions', 'C. Increased traffic and slower performance', 'D. No noticeable change', 'C', 'Collisions cause retransmissions, which increase the traffic load and degrade performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: How can network managers determine if switching is necessary?', 'A. By analyzing packet headers', 'B. By using network utilities to measure utilization and collision rates', 'C. By installing more switches blindly', 'D. By observing the blinking lights on switches', 'B', 'Network managers can assess performance needs by examining real-time statistics using standard tools.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill: The number of packets with collisions as a percentage of total packets is known as the _______.', 'A. Utilization load', 'B. Collision rate', 'C. Buffer capacity', 'D. Throughput ratio', 'B', 'Collision rate is defined as the percentage of total packets that experience collisions.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpretation: If a network has a utilization rate of 40% and a collision rate of 15%, what does this suggest?', 'A. The network is underutilized', 'B. Switching would not help performance', 'C. The network is a good candidate for switching', 'D. Network devices should be replaced', 'C', 'Both high utilization and high collision rates indicate congestion, where switching can significantly enhance performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a key benefit of replacing a central hub with a switch in a network?', 'A. Increased number of cables required', 'B. Reduced server performance', 'C. Faster connections and traffic isolation', 'D. Increased collision rates', 'C', 'Switches improve performance by reducing unnecessary traffic and offering dedicated bandwidth to each device.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Replacing hubs with switches eliminates the network bottleneck permanently.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Switching removes the existing bottleneck, but performance grows until a new bottleneck (like server limits) is reached.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a common method for relieving congestion in a switched network?', 'A. Disconnecting half the devices', 'B. Replacing switches with hubs', 'C. Adding more switched ports or increasing their speed', 'D. Installing firewalls on each segment', 'C', 'Congestion is typically resolved by increasing port capacity or further segmenting high-use areas.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which performance metrics are used to identify congested network segments?', 'A. Server load and cable length', 'B. Signal strength and latency', 'C. Utilization and collision rates', 'D. IP address range and subnet mask', 'C', 'These metrics indicate how heavily a segment is used and if packets are being lost due to collisions.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fast Ethernet is typically used to:', 'A. Connect low-priority devices', 'B. Decrease server processing power', 'C. Connect switches and critical servers', 'D. Eliminate the need for segmentation', 'C', 'Fast Ethernet ensures high-speed links between performance-critical parts of the network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Most network design changes are revolutionary and done from scratch.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Changes are usually evolutionary to preserve existing investments while upgrading key components.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes a common approach to updating outdated network technology?', 'A. Immediate replacement of the entire network', 'B. Gradual upgrades with switch and bridge integration', 'C. Adding wireless access points only', 'D. Ignoring old systems and starting anew', 'B', 'Bridges and switches help integrate new technology with legacy systems for smoother transitions.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the purpose of adding Ethernet switch ports further down the network tree?', 'A. To increase security between departments', 'B. To reduce cabling requirements', 'C. To increase network performance', 'D. To improve wireless coverage', 'C', 'Strategically placed switch ports help alleviate congestion and enhance data flow in busy segments.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT a reason to upgrade a network gradually?', 'A. Preserve usable capital investment', 'B. Avoid downtime from total replacement', 'C. Completely eliminate outdated systems instantly', 'D. Integrate with existing infrastructure', 'C', 'Immediate elimination is costly and impractical; gradual upgrades are more feasible and cost-effective.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why is Fast Ethernet easy to integrate into existing networks?', 'A. It requires no switches', 'B. It works only with new infrastructure', 'C. It connects through bridges and switches to existing Ethernet', 'D. It eliminates the need for routers', 'C', 'Fast Ethernet can interoperate with older Ethernet setups using bridges or switches, making upgrades seamless.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a major benefit of using Fast Ethernet with switched Ethernet in client/server networks?', 'A. Increases server RAM', 'B. Prevents malware attacks', 'C. Allows servers to use faster ports, reducing bottlenecks', 'D. Reduces server power consumption', 'C', 'Fast Ethernet with switching enables servers to connect via high-speed ports, preventing LAN bottlenecks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Distributed processing does not benefit from network segmentation using switches.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Distributed processing benefits greatly from switches and Fast Ethernet, which improve traffic flow by segmenting networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following helps improve performance in a switched Ethernet network?', 'A. Grouping unrelated users together', 'B. Ignoring traffic patterns', 'C. Using slow uplinks for switches', 'D. Segmenting users by communication patterns', 'D', 'Grouping users who communicate often onto the same segment reduces cross-segment traffic and increases efficiency.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How should switch usage be optimized to prevent network bottlenecks?', 'A. Use hubs instead of switches', 'B. Use fast uplinks between switches', 'C. Avoid using multiple switches', 'D. Use only one switch for all nodes', 'B', 'Fast uplinks prevent bottlenecks between switches, allowing better performance across segments.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What network utilization threshold is suggested for optimizing switch performance?', 'A. 10%', 'B. 50%', 'C. 35%', 'D. 75%', 'C', 'To optimize performance, switches should ideally have less than 35% utilization per node.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: What type of Ethernet is commonly used as a backbone in switched networks?', 'A. Token Ring', 'B. Gigabit Ethernet', 'C. Fast Ethernet', 'D. Wireless Ethernet', 'C', 'Fast Ethernet provides sufficient speed for backbone connections in switched networks at a lower cost.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT a key recommendation when optimizing network switching performance?', 'A. Move users between segments iteratively', 'B. Group nodes based on communication patterns', 'C. Ignore departmental traffic patterns', 'D. Keep node utilization under 35%', 'C', 'Departmental traffic patterns are essential for segment design and improving network performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill: A ______ is created when too many clients attempt to access a single server on the LAN.', 'A. firewall', 'B. packet storm', 'C. bottleneck', 'D. loop', 'C', 'When too many clients access a server, the traffic overloads the connection, creating a bottleneck.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is one major reason to iteratively move users between segments in a switched network?', 'A. To save IP addresses', 'B. To balance the utilization across all nodes', 'C. To increase hardware costs', 'D. To reduce wireless interference', 'B', 'Balancing traffic ensures each segment stays below optimal utilization thresholds.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Advanced switching technology issues affect most typical business networks.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Most advanced switching issues do not impact over 95% of standard networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which protocol allows switches to coordinate redundant links to prevent traffic loops in a network?', 'A. SNMP', 'B. VLAN', 'C. RMON', 'D. Spanning Tree', 'D', 'Spanning Tree is a protocol used to prevent loops in networks with redundant links by coordinating traffic paths among switches.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary benefit of using VLANs in managed switches?', 'A. Physical separation of devices', 'B. Grouping devices logically for better broadcast/multicast traffic management', 'C. Increased cabling requirements', 'D. Reduced power consumption', 'B', 'VLANs allow logical grouping of devices, optimizing traffic and reducing unnecessary broadcast and multicast flooding.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Unmanaged switches can filter multicast and broadcast traffic by default.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Unmanaged switches forward all multicast and broadcast traffic to all ports without filtering.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is an extension of SNMP that provides more data with less bandwidth usage?', 'A. SMTP', 'B. RMON', 'C. STP', 'D. VLAN', 'B', 'RMON is an extension of SNMP that offers more detailed network monitoring using less bandwidth.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Networks with mission-critical applications often use __________ switches for better control and monitoring.', 'A. unmanaged', 'B. dumb', 'C. managed', 'D. passive', 'C', 'Managed switches offer features like SNMP, VLANs, and Spanning Tree which are essential for mission-critical networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Short: Why would a network administrator prefer managed switches in a large enterprise?', 'A. They are cheaper', 'B. They support advanced features like VLANs, SNMP, and Spanning Tree', 'C. They consume more power', 'D. They are plug and play', 'B', 'These features help monitor health, optimize traffic, and prevent loops in large networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What happens when switches form a loop without Spanning Tree enabled?', 'A. Faster transmission of packets', 'B. Improved redundancy with no risk', 'C. Traffic loops and broadcast storms', 'D. Lower network load', 'C', 'Without Spanning Tree, loops cause repeated traffic circulation leading to broadcast storms.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT a feature of an unmanaged switch?', 'A. Plug and play', 'B. VLAN configuration', 'C. No SNMP support', 'D. Pass-through of all broadcast traffic', 'B', 'VLANs are only supported in managed switches. Unmanaged switches lack configuration features.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Managed switches are essential for implementing VLANs and supporting network monitoring tools.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Managed switches are required for VLANs and support protocols like SNMP for monitoring.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Interpretation: A network has a logical layout that differs from its physical cable layout. Which switch type is most suitable?', 'A. Unmanaged switch', 'B. Passive hub', 'C. Managed switch with VLAN support', 'D. Router with no switch', 'C', 'When logical and physical groupings differ, VLAN support in managed switches enables efficient segmentation.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the key difference between cut-through and store-and-forward switches?', 'A. Cut-through switches inspect the entire packet, store-and-forward only the header.', 'B. Store-and-forward switches only use full-duplex communication.', 'C. Cut-through switches examine only the destination address, store-and-forward switches examine the entire packet.', 'D. Store-and-forward switches are faster than cut-through switches.', 'C', 'Cut-through switches prioritize speed by checking only the destination address, while store-and-forward switches inspect the entire packet for errors before forwarding.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why might a network administrator prefer a store-and-forward switch over a cut-through switch?', 'A. They are cheaper.', 'B. They forward packets without delay.', 'C. They help prevent propagation of bad packets through the network.', 'D. They support only single duplex connections.', 'C', 'By examining the entire packet, store-and-forward switches can detect errors and collisions before forwarding data, ensuring more reliable transmission.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Cut-through switches can catch packet errors before forwarding.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Cut-through switches do not examine the entire packet and therefore cannot detect packet errors like store-and-forward switches can.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which switch type has seen improvements that reduce the performance gap with the other?', 'A. Cut-through', 'B. Store-and-forward', 'C. Hybrid', 'D. Non-blocking', 'B', 'Store-and-forward switches have improved in speed and now rival cut-through switches in performance while offering better error detection.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary factor that classifies a switch as blocking?', 'A. It doesn''t allow VLAN tagging', 'B. It only supports half-duplex connections', 'C. Its internal switching components cannot handle full theoretical throughput', 'D. It doesn''t use error-checking algorithms', 'C', 'A blocking switch cannot manage the full theoretical traffic load across all its ports simultaneously due to hardware limitations.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: All switches should be designed as non-blocking for optimal performance in all networks.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Non-blocking switches are ideal but costly; blocking switches are sufficient for most smaller networks due to lower usage demands.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How much switching bus speed is typically adequate for an 8-port 10/100 full-duplex switch with average 50% utilization?', 'A. 1.6 Gbps', 'B. 800 Mbps', 'C. 400 Mbps', 'D. 100 Mbps', 'B', 'With each port capable of 200 Mbps and 50% utilization assumed, 8 × 100 Mbps = 800 Mbps is typically sufficient.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does the term ''non-blocking switch'' imply?', 'A. The switch blocks all traffic during configuration', 'B. The switch can handle all port traffic at full theoretical capacity simultaneously', 'C. The switch uses store-and-forward logic exclusively', 'D. The switch does not allow broadcast messages', 'B', 'A non-blocking switch is capable of processing the full traffic load from all ports without congestion or dropping packets.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT an advantage of store-and-forward switches?', 'A. Error checking', 'B. Reduced collisions', 'C. Lower latency than cut-through', 'D. Improved reliability', 'C', 'Store-and-forward switches typically have higher latency because they examine the entire packet, unlike cut-through switches that forward data more quickly.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why are hybrid switches becoming more common in modern networks?', 'A. They are more expensive and only used in labs', 'B. They support only one mode of switching', 'C. They combine the advantages of cut-through and store-and-forward switching', 'D. They eliminate the need for network protocols', 'C', 'Hybrid switches allow for faster data forwarding with cut-through logic when possible, while switching to store-and-forward for error checking when needed.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What happens when a switch buffer becomes full due to congestion on the destination segment?', 'A. The switch immediately forwards the packet regardless', 'B. The switch holds the packet until bandwidth is available', 'C. The switch disables the port', 'D. The switch redirects the packet to another network', 'B', 'Switches buffer packets temporarily if the destination segment is congested, waiting for bandwidth to free up before forwarding.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is a strategy switches use to handle full buffers?', 'A. Send packets upstream to source nodes (backpressure flow control)', 'B. Forward packets without delay', 'C. Drop the packet and rely on network retransmission', 'D. Both A and C', 'D', 'Switches may either send backpressure signals upstream or drop packets to trigger retransmission; both strategies have trade-offs.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Backpressure flow control solves the problem of network congestion completely.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'Backpressure only spreads congestion to upstream segments and does not eliminate the root cause of congested segments.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why do switch vendors recommend designing network topologies to eliminate congested segments?', 'A. To reduce the need for large buffers', 'B. To improve overall network performance', 'C. To avoid retransmissions and delays', 'D. All of the above', 'D', 'Eliminating congestion reduces buffering, delays, and retransmissions, improving network efficiency.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What layers of the OSI model do multilayer switches operate on?', 'A. Layer 1 only', 'B. Layers 2 and 3', 'C. Layers 4 and 5', 'D. Layer 3 only', 'B', 'Multilayer switches combine switching at Layer 2 and routing functions at Layer 3 for enhanced performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How do multilayer switches optimize network traffic handling?', 'A. By switching all packets at the hardware layer regardless of type', 'B. By routing all packets through software processing', 'C. By switching common traffic flows in hardware and routing other traffic as needed', 'D. By dropping broadcast packets', 'C', 'Multilayer switches handle frequent traffic quickly via hardware switching and use routing only for exceptional cases.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Multilayer switches are expected to eventually replace routers in most large networks.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Because of their speed and routing capabilities, multilayer switches are likely to replace routers in large enterprise networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary benefit of using large buffers in switches?', 'A. To increase switch price', 'B. To hold packets during temporary congestion', 'C. To slow down the network', 'D. To enable faster packet processing', 'B', 'Large buffers temporarily store packets when the destination segment is congested to avoid immediate packet loss.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the downside of dropping packets when switch buffers are full?', 'A. It reduces network latency', 'B. It increases retransmissions, causing higher network load', 'C. It speeds up traffic', 'D. It prevents packet loss', 'B', 'Dropped packets must be resent, which increases network load and can worsen congestion.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the main function of Layer 3 switching compared to Layer 2 switching?', 'A. Switching by MAC addresses only', 'B. Routing packets based on IP addresses', 'C. Operating only on physical layer signals', 'D. Repeating Ethernet frames without inspection', 'B', 'Layer 3 switching includes routing functions, allowing decisions based on IP addresses, unlike Layer 2 which uses MAC addresses.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is Unshielded Twisted Pair (UTP) cable primarily used for?', 'A. Networking and traditional telephone wiring', 'B. Power transmission', 'C. Coaxial cable replacement only', 'D. Fiber optic connections', 'A', 'UTP cables are widely used for both network data transmission and telephone lines.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How many categories of UTP cable are commonly recognized?', 'A. 3', 'B. 5', 'C. 7', 'D. 10', 'C', 'There are seven commonly recognized categories of UTP cables, ranging from Category 1 to Category 7.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which UTP category is the most popular for modern networking?', 'A. CAT3', 'B. CAT5e', 'C. CAT6', 'D. CAT7', 'B', 'CAT5e replaced older coaxial cables and is widely used for fast and reliable network connections.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: The numbers in UTP categories represent the number of twists inside the wire.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'The category numbers relate to the specification revision and correlate with the number and quality of twists in the cable.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is one key characteristic of UTP cables that makes them easy to work with?', 'A. They require complex installation tools', 'B. They are easy to install, expand, and troubleshoot', 'C. They are heavily shielded', 'D. They only support low-speed data', 'B', 'UTP cables are designed to be user-friendly for installation and maintenance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which cable type did CAT5e UTP cable replace due to faster and more reliable networking?', 'A. Coaxial cable', 'B. Fiber optic cable', 'C. Shielded twisted pair (STP)', 'D. Power cables', 'A', 'CAT5e UTP replaced older coaxial cables that couldn''t support modern network speeds.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: Most telephone and network wires are made of _____.', 'A. Aluminum', 'B. Copper', 'C. Fiber optic', 'D. Steel', 'B', 'Copper is the standard conductor material used in telephone and network cables.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which UTP category is generally used for traditional telephone wiring?', 'A. CAT1', 'B. CAT3', 'C. CAT5', 'D. CAT7', 'A', 'UTP-CAT1 was originally used for telephone wiring, before networking became common.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why does the category number in UTP cables matter?', 'A. It indicates the cable length', 'B. It defines the type of copper wire and jack compatibility', 'C. It specifies the color of the cable', 'D. It shows the cable''s price', 'B', 'The category number refers to specifications that include wire type and connectors.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: UTP cables can be used for both networking and telephone applications.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'UTP cables are versatile and serve in both data networks and traditional telephone systems.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What type of wiring is CAT1 used for?', 'A. Telephone wiring only', 'B. Computer network wiring', 'C. Fiber optic networks', 'D. Power transmission', 'A', 'CAT1 wire is not twisted and supports only telephone services such as ISDN and PSTN, not computer networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which UTP category is mostly used for token ring networks supporting speeds up to 4 Mbps?', 'A. CAT1', 'B. CAT2', 'C. CAT5e', 'D. CAT6', 'B', 'CAT2 cable supports token ring networks up to 4 Mbps.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: CAT5e cable is more forgiving than higher category cables when installation guidelines are not perfectly followed.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'CAT5e cables tolerate some deviations in termination and deployment better than higher category cables.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the maximum length CAT6 can support 10Gbps speeds?', 'A. 100 meters', 'B. 55 meters', 'C. 200 meters', 'D. 10 meters', 'B', 'CAT6 supports 10Gbps speeds up to 55 meters, after which performance decreases.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which category cable introduced in 2009 offers better crosstalk and electromagnetic interference immunity than CAT6?', 'A. CAT5e', 'B. CAT6A', 'C. CAT7', 'D. CAT3', 'B', 'CAT6A is an enhanced version of CAT6, designed for better shielding and reduced interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which cable category contains a physical separator between wire pairs to reduce interference?', 'A. CAT3', 'B. CAT5', 'C. CAT6', 'D. CAT7', 'C', 'CAT6 cables include a separator to reduce electromagnetic interference between wire pairs.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Fill in the blank: CAT3 and CAT4 cables are used mainly for _____ networks.', 'A. Ethernet', 'B. Token Ring', 'C. Fiber Optic', 'D. Wi-Fi', 'B', 'CAT3 and CAT4 were commonly used in Token Ring networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which cable has more twists per inch, CAT3 or CAT5?', 'A. CAT3', 'B. CAT5', 'C. Both have the same twists', 'D. Neither has twists', 'B', 'CAT5 cables have more twists per inch than CAT3, allowing higher speed and longer runs.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: CAT6 cabling requires all components, including jacks and patch panels, to be certified CAT6 for proper performance.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Proper performance of CAT6 cabling depends on certified components and correct termination.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which UTP cable category supports gigabit Ethernet speeds for lengths up to 100 meters?', 'A. CAT5e', 'B. CAT6', 'C. CAT4', 'D. CAT1', 'B', 'CAT6 supports gigabit Ethernet speeds for distances up to 100 meters.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the maximum speed supported by CAT7 cable at lengths up to 100 meters?', 'A. 1 Gbps', 'B. 10 Gbps', 'C. 100 Mbps', 'D. 100 Gbps', 'B', 'CAT7 is designed to support 10Gbps speeds at distances up to 100 meters.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How many individually shielded pairs does a CAT7 cable have?', 'A. 2', 'B. 3', 'C. 4', 'D. 5', 'C', 'CAT7 cables have four individually shielded pairs plus an overall cable shield.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why is it important to use only CAT7 certified components throughout a CAT7 installation?', 'A. To reduce cable weight', 'B. To maintain cable flexibility', 'C. To ensure performance standards and pass certification tests', 'D. To reduce installation cost', 'C', 'Using non-certified components can degrade performance and cause failure of certification tests.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: CAT7 is commonly used in home networking setups.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'CAT7 is typically used in data centers and backbone connections rather than home networks.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the core of a fiber optic cable made of?', 'A. Copper wire', 'B. Plastic or glass fibers', 'C. Steel strands', 'D. Aluminum foil', 'B', 'Fiber optic cables have cores made of glass or plastic fibers for transmitting light.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the purpose of the cladding layer in fiber optic cables?', 'A. To protect the cable from physical damage', 'B. To reflect light back into the core allowing the fiber to bend', 'C. To carry electrical signals', 'D. To provide color coding for fibers', 'B', 'Cladding has a different density than the core and reflects light back, enabling signal transmission even when bent.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What material surrounds the cladding in fiber optic cables to provide strength?', 'A. Steel wire', 'B. Kevlar (polymeric fiber) strands', 'C. Copper shielding', 'D. Plastic sheath', 'B', 'Kevlar strands add tensile strength and protect the delicate fibers inside.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: The plastic sheath in fiber optic cables is opaque to absorb escaping light.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'The plastic sheath absorbs any light that escapes, preventing signal loss and external interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What type of light sources are used to send data through fiber optic cables?', 'A. Incandescent bulbs', 'B. Fluorescent lamps', 'C. Lasers or LEDs', 'D. Infrared heaters', 'C', 'Fiber optic cables transmit data by pulsing light from lasers or LEDs through the core.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Where is CAT7 cable most commonly deployed today?', 'A. Residential home networks', 'B. Data centers for backbone connections', 'C. Wireless networks', 'D. Satellite communications', 'B', 'Due to its high performance, CAT7 is primarily used in data centers to connect servers and switches.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is a benefit of fiber optic cabling over copper cabling?', 'A. Lower cost', 'B. Extremely high throughput', 'C. Easier to splice', 'D. Heavier cable weight', 'B', 'Fiber optic cables offer extremely high throughput compared to copper cabling.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Fiber optic cables are highly resistant to noise and electromagnetic interference (EMI).', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Fiber optic cables are unaffected by EMI, providing very high noise resistance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a major drawback of fiber optic cabling compared to twisted pair copper cables?', 'A. Lower data transmission rates', 'B. Less security', 'C. More expensive', 'D. Shorter maximum segment length', 'C', 'Fiber optic cabling is generally more expensive than twisted pair copper cables.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of these is NOT a typical benefit of fiber optic cables?', 'A. Ability to carry signals over longer distances', 'B. Industry standard for high-speed networking', 'C. Susceptibility to electromagnetic interference', 'D. Excellent security', 'C', 'Fiber optic cables are immune to electromagnetic interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How long can fiber optic cable segments typically range?', 'A. 1 to 100 meters', 'B. 150 to 40,000 meters', 'C. 100 to 500 meters', 'D. 50 to 1000 meters', 'B', 'Segment length depends on the light’s wavelength and type of cable, varying from 150 to 40,000 meters.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What determines the segment length of a fiber optic cable?', 'A. The cable''s color', 'B. The type of connectors used', 'C. The light’s wavelength and type of cable', 'D. The installation environment temperature', 'C', 'The segment length varies based on the light’s wavelength and the specific fiber optic cable type.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Fiber optic cabling is the industry standard for high-speed networking.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Fiber optic cabling is widely accepted as the industry standard for high-speed networking.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is a drawback of fiber optic cabling?', 'A. Requires special equipment to splice', 'B. Highly susceptible to electromagnetic interference', 'C. Limited security', 'D. Low throughput', 'A', 'Splicing fiber optic cables requires special equipment and skills.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why is fiber optic cabling considered secure?', 'A. Because it uses light signals that are difficult to tap without detection.', 'B. Because it uses electrical signals that are easy to monitor.', 'C. Because it is made of copper wires.', 'D. Because it broadcasts signals openly.', 'A', 'The transmission of data as light pulses in fiber optics makes it resistant to tapping and interference, enhancing security.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the maximum data rate fiber optic cables can reliably transmit per channel?', 'A. 10 Mbps', 'B. 100 Mbps', 'C. 1 Gbps', 'D. 100 Gbps', 'D', 'Fiber optic cables have been proven reliable in transmitting data rates up to 100 gigabits per second per channel.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the typical core diameter of Single Mode Fiber (SMF)?', 'A. 8-10 microns', 'B. 50 microns', 'C. 62.5 microns', 'D. 100 microns', 'A', 'SMF has a narrow core diameter of about 8-10 microns.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How does laser-generated light travel in Single Mode Fiber?', 'A. Over multiple paths', 'B. Over one path', 'C. Through LED pulses', 'D. Through fiber cladding', 'B', 'In SMF, laser-generated light travels over a single path, reducing reflection and dispersion.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why is Single Mode Fiber rarely used for shorter connections?', 'A. Due to high attenuation', 'B. Due to cost', 'C. Due to large core size', 'D. Due to low data rates', 'B', 'SMF is more expensive and therefore rarely used for short connections.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: The internet backbone largely depends on Single Mode Fiber.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'SMF supports long distance transmission with little signal loss, making it ideal for backbone infrastructure.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a common core diameter for Multimode Fiber (MMF)?', 'A. 8-10 microns', 'B. 50 or 62.5 microns', 'C. 100 microns', 'D. 200 microns', 'B', 'MMF typically has a larger core diameter of 50 or 62.5 microns.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which type of light source is commonly used with Multimode Fiber?', 'A. Laser only', 'B. LED only', 'C. Laser or LED', 'D. Incandescent', 'C', 'MMF uses either laser or LED light pulses that travel at different angles.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Compared to Single Mode Fiber, Multimode Fiber experiences:', 'A. Less attenuation', 'B. Greater attenuation', 'C. Same attenuation', 'D. No attenuation', 'B', 'MMF suffers from greater attenuation due to multiple light paths and reflections.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Typical uses for Multimode Fiber include:', 'A. Long-distance backbone connections', 'B. Cables connecting routers to switches', 'C. Telephone wiring', 'D. Wireless networking', 'B', 'MMF is commonly used for shorter runs such as connecting routers to switches or servers in a network backbone.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Light disperses significantly as it travels in Single Mode Fiber.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'B', 'In SMF, light does not disperse much due to single path transmission.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which fiber type has a narrower core and supports longer distance transmission?', 'A. Multimode Fiber', 'B. Single Mode Fiber', 'C. Coaxial Cable', 'D. Twisted Pair Cable', 'B', 'SMF’s narrow core and single light path enable longer distance transmission with low signal loss.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which factor primarily classifies multimode fiber (MMF) connectors?', 'A. Size of the ferrule', 'B. Number of fibers', 'C. Type of polishing', 'D. Connector shape', 'B', 'MMF connectors are classified mainly by how many fibers they connect, distinguishing them from single-mode fiber connectors.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the ferrule in fiber optic connectors?', 'A. The protective outer casing', 'B. The fiber core', 'C. The extended tip that contacts the jack receptacle', 'D. A type of connector polish', 'C', 'The ferrule is the part of the connector that physically interfaces with the jack to ensure proper alignment and connection.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following polishing types is used to reduce back reflection in SMF connectors?', 'A. Physical Contact (PC)', 'B. Ultra-Polished Connector (UPC)', 'C. Angle Polished Connector (APC)', 'D. All of the above', 'D', 'SMF connectors use PC, UPC, and APC polishing techniques to minimize signal loss due to back reflection.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which ferrule size is most common for the LC connector in SMF?', 'A. 0.5 mm', 'B. 1.25 mm', 'C. 2.5 mm', 'D. 5 mm', 'B', 'The LC connector typically uses a 1.25-mm ferrule, making it smaller than the other common SMF connectors.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Identify the three common 2.5-mm ferrule SMF connectors.', 'A. LC, MT-RJ, SC', 'B. SC, ST, FC', 'C. PC, UPC, APC', 'D. RJ-45, LC, ST', 'B', 'SC, ST, and FC connectors use 2.5-mm ferrules and are common in SMF setups.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which MMF connector is commonly used on the latest fiber-optic technology?', 'A. ST', 'B. SC', 'C. LC and MT-RJ', 'D. FC', 'C', 'LC and MT-RJ connectors are more compact and widely used in newer fiber optic installations.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary purpose of fiber-optic converters?', 'A. To increase cable length', 'B. To connect multimode fiber to single-mode fiber networks', 'C. To polish fiber connectors', 'D. To replace copper cables with fiber', 'B', 'Converters enable interoperability between different fiber types and between fiber and copper parts of a network.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does a bidirectional fiber-optic converter do?', 'A. Amplifies signals only', 'B. Accepts, regenerates, and forwards signals between different network segments', 'C. Changes signal frequency', 'D. Converts electrical signals to optical signals', 'B', 'Bidirectional converters ensure smooth data flow by receiving and regenerating signals for the next network part.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a GBIC in fiber-optic networking?', 'A. A cable type', 'B. A modular interface standard for Gigabit Ethernet', 'C. A connector polish', 'D. A fiber cable tester', 'B', 'GBICs are modular transceivers that provide flexible connection options for Gigabit Ethernet.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How do Small Form-factor Pluggable (SFP) transceivers differ from GBICs?', 'A. SFPs support lower speeds', 'B. SFPs allow more ports per inch and are smaller', 'C. SFPs are larger and more expensive', 'D. SFPs are used only for copper cables', 'B', 'SFP transceivers are miniaturized versions of GBICs, enabling higher port density on networking equipment.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the purpose of a loopback adapter in fiber-optic networking?', 'A. To amplify signals over long distances', 'B. To test an SFP’s functionality or check for a mismatch', 'C. To connect two fiber cables together', 'D. To convert fiber signals to electrical signals', 'B', 'Loopback adapters are used for diagnostic testing to verify that SFP transceivers and connections work correctly.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How is an SFP typically installed on a network device?', 'A. By soldering onto the circuit board', 'B. Sliding into a keyed socket with a tab or latch', 'C. Using a screw mount on the device', 'D. Plugging directly into an RJ-45 port', 'B', 'SFP modules are designed to slide into a socket with keyed alignment and often have a tab or latch to secure them.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is electromagnetic interference (EMI)?', 'A. A signal sent intentionally to improve transmission', 'B. An undesirable influence degrading or distorting signal', 'C. A type of connector used in fiber optics', 'D. A protocol for managing network traffic', 'B', 'EMI refers to unwanted electromagnetic signals that interfere with the proper transmission of data.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is cross-talk in network cabling?', 'A. Signal on one wire infringing on an adjacent wire''s signal', 'B. A method of encrypting data', 'C. A type of fiber optic connector', 'D. A protocol for switching', 'A', 'Cross-talk occurs when signals from one wire affect another, causing interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does alien cross-talk refer to?', 'A. Interference within the same cable pair', 'B. Interference between two different cables', 'C. Interference caused by wireless devices', 'D. A special type of signal boost', 'B', 'Alien cross-talk happens when signals leak from one cable to another adjacent cable.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Where does Near End Cross-Talk (NEXT) typically occur?', 'A. Near the signal destination', 'B. Near the signal source', 'C. Midway between source and destination', 'D. Only in wireless transmissions', 'B', 'NEXT occurs near the source end of the cable where signals interfere with each other.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why is it important to minimize EMI and cross-talk in network cabling?', 'A. To reduce data transmission errors', 'B. To increase physical cable length', 'C. To speed up hardware installation', 'D. To improve device aesthetics', 'A', 'EMI and cross-talk cause signal degradation, leading to errors and reduced network performance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which tool is useful to verify SFP module installation and function?', 'A. Cable tester', 'B. Loopback adapter', 'C. Multimeter', 'D. Network analyzer', 'B', 'A loopback adapter is used specifically to test SFP modules by looping signals back for verification.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the typical form factor used for modern fiber optic transceivers to maximize port density?', 'A. GBIC', 'B. SFP', 'C. RJ-45', 'D. ST connector', 'B', 'SFP transceivers are smaller than GBICs and allow more ports per unit area.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT a cause of noise in data transmission?', 'A. Electromagnetic interference (EMI)', 'B. Cross-talk', 'C. Alien cross-talk', 'D. Fiber optic cabling', 'D', 'Fiber optic cables are immune to EMI and cross-talk, so they do not cause noise.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is Far End Cross-Talk (FEXT)?', 'A. Interference near the signal source', 'B. Interference occurring at the far end of a cable', 'C. Noise caused by faulty connectors', 'D. Signal loss due to cable attenuation', 'B', 'FEXT happens at the far end of the cable opposite the signal source, causing interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does attenuation refer to in networking?', 'A. Increase in signal strength over distance', 'B. Loss of signal strength as it travels from source', 'C. Delay caused by repeaters', 'D. Crosstalk between cables', 'B', 'Attenuation is the natural loss of signal strength as it travels over a medium.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How does an amplifier affect analog signals?', 'A. Regenerates the original digital signal without noise', 'B. Increases voltage but may also amplify noise', 'C. Eliminates all noise from the signal', 'D. Converts analog signals to digital', 'B', 'Amplifiers boost signal strength but also amplify any noise present.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the role of a repeater in digital networks?', 'A. Amplify analog signals with noise', 'B. Convert signals from fiber to copper', 'C. Regenerate digital signals in original form without accumulated noise', 'D. Delay transmission for synchronization', 'C', 'Repeaters clean and restore digital signals to their original state, removing noise.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is latency in a network?', 'A. Loss of signal strength over distance', 'B. Time delay between sending and receiving a signal', 'C. Noise interference from other cables', 'D. Distance a cable covers', 'B', 'Latency measures the delay from transmission to receipt of a signal.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which factors contribute to latency?', 'A. Cable length and intervening connectivity devices', 'B. Signal frequency and wavelength', 'C. Connector type and cable shielding', 'D. Noise and electromagnetic interference', 'A', 'Long cables and devices like switches increase signal travel time, adding latency.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is round trip time (RTT) in networking?', 'A. Time for a packet to travel one way from sender to receiver', 'B. Time for a packet to go from sender to receiver and back', 'C. Time to process a packet at the destination', 'D. Time delay due to signal attenuation', 'B', 'RTT is the total time for a signal to travel to the receiver and for the response to return.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which is NOT a common fiber cable problem?', 'A. Fiber type mismatch', 'B. Wavelength mismatch', 'C. Dirty connectors', 'D. Electrical interference', 'D', 'Fiber optic cables are immune to electrical interference, unlike copper cables.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why can even same-mode fiber cables be mismatched?', 'A. Different cable lengths', 'B. Different core widths', 'C. Different jacket materials', 'D. Different connector types', 'B', 'Fiber cables with differing core diameters may not properly transmit signals despite having the same mode.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the function of a tone generator (toner) in network troubleshooting?', 'A. Measures voltage in a cable', 'B. Issues a signal on a wire pair to help trace cables', 'C. Detects the length of a cable', 'D. Analyzes data traffic on the network', 'B', 'Tone generators send a signal through wires to help identify and trace cable paths.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does a tone locator (probe) do?', 'A. Sends signals on cables', 'B. Emits a tone when electrical activity is detected', 'C. Measures resistance in a circuit', 'D. Detects wireless network signals', 'B', 'Tone locators detect tones from tone generators by emitting sound when signal is found.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is a probe kit or toner probe?', 'A. A device for testing cable length', 'B. A combination of tone generator and tone locator used to trace cables', 'C. A tool for measuring voltage in circuits', 'D. A wireless signal analyzer', 'B', 'Probe kits include both signal generation and detection to help find cable endpoints.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why does testing cables with toner probes often require trial and error?', 'A. Because signal strength is always weak', 'B. Because cable pairs may be difficult to identify without visual aids', 'C. Because the probes have limited battery life', 'D. Because it requires network configuration', 'B', 'Testing requires trial and error since tone signals can be faint and pairs must be traced carefully.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary purpose of a multimeter in network troubleshooting?', 'A. To send test tones on cables', 'B. To measure voltage, resistance, and impedance in cables', 'C. To analyze data packets on the network', 'D. To detect wireless interference', 'B', 'Multimeters test electrical characteristics to ensure proper cable function and identify faults.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How can a multimeter help verify cable integrity?', 'A. By measuring the cable length', 'B. By measuring voltage and checking for shorts or open circuits', 'C. By sending a tone through the cable', 'D. By analyzing network traffic', 'B', 'Multimeters detect voltage presence and continuity to ensure cables are intact and functional.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT a use of a multimeter in cable testing?', 'A. Measuring voltage', 'B. Checking for noise presence', 'C. Sending a tone signal', 'D. Testing resistance', 'C', 'Sending tone signals is done by tone generators, not multimeters.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A tone generator can determine cable characteristics such as impedance or resistance.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Sometimes', 'D. Only with additional equipment', 'B', 'Tone generators only send signals for tracing cables and do not analyze cable characteristics.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the main limitation of using tone locator and generator kits for troubleshooting?', 'A. They cannot trace cables over long distances', 'B. They require trial and error and cannot determine cable quality', 'C. They are only usable with fiber optic cables', 'D. They cannot detect shorts in the wire', 'B', 'Tone kits help locate cables but do not provide detailed information on cable condition.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which tool would you use to check for open or short circuits in a cable?', 'A. Tone generator', 'B. Tone locator', 'C. Multimeter', 'D. Cable tester', 'C', 'Multimeters measure electrical continuity and resistance, helping detect shorts and open circuits.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary function of a cable continuity tester?', 'A. To measure network speed', 'B. To test whether a cable carries a signal to its destination', 'C. To configure network devices', 'D. To encrypt data on cables', 'B', 'Cable continuity testers verify if a cable is properly connected and signals can pass through.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What are the two main parts of a copper-based cable tester?', 'A. Transmitter and receiver', 'B. Base unit generating voltage and remote unit detecting voltage', 'C. Signal analyzer and display screen', 'D. Connector and cable', 'B', 'Copper cable testers work by sending voltage from a base unit to a remote unit to check continuity.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How do cable continuity testers typically indicate a pass or fail result?', 'A. Through a series of lights and audible tones', 'B. Via digital display messages', 'C. By sending network packets', 'D. Using temperature sensors', 'A', 'Visual lights and tones provide simple feedback on cable status for quick troubleshooting.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What additional feature do some continuity testers verify in UTP or STP cables?', 'A. Cable color coding', 'B. Wire pairing correctness', 'C. Cable length', 'D. Signal encryption', 'B', 'Correct pairing is important to avoid crosstalk and ensure proper cable function.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How does a fiber optic continuity tester check the cable?', 'A. By sending electrical signals', 'B. By issuing light pulses through the fiber', 'C. By measuring cable resistance', 'D. By detecting heat signatures', 'B', 'Fiber optic cables transmit light, so testing involves sending and detecting light pulses.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why should all cables be tested to ensure they meet network standards?', 'A. To reduce electromagnetic interference', 'B. To ensure reliable network performance and compliance', 'C. To decrease cable costs', 'D. To simplify installation', 'B', 'Testing verifies cables meet specifications required for network speed and reliability.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the benefit of cable testers being portable, lightweight, and low cost?', 'A. They can be used only by network engineers', 'B. They can be easily carried and used on-site for quick troubleshooting', 'C. They are disposable after one use', 'D. They work without batteries', 'B', 'Portability allows technicians to efficiently test cables in various locations.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which device can measure the distance to a cable fault or connectivity device?', 'A. Basic cable continuity tester', 'B. Cable performance tester or certifier', 'C. Tone generator', 'D. Multimeter', 'B', 'Advanced testers can measure cable length and locate faults to facilitate repairs.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following is NOT a function of a cable performance tester?', 'A. Measure near end cross-talk', 'B. Measure attenuation', 'C. Issue pass/fail ratings for Cat 3-7 standards', 'D. Encrypt network data', 'D', 'Cable testers evaluate physical cable properties but do not perform data encryption.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: A cable performance tester can verify termination resistance and impedance.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Sometimes', 'D. Only with special attachments', 'A', 'Performance testers check electrical properties like resistance and impedance to ensure proper cable function.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary function of an Optical Power Meter (OPM)?', 'A. To measure the amount of light power transmitted on a fiber-optic line', 'B. To amplify the light signal in fiber-optic cables', 'C. To splice fiber-optic cables', 'D. To convert optical signals to electrical signals', 'A', 'OPMs measure the strength of light signals to assess the performance of fiber-optic cables.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why must an Optical Power Meter be precisely calibrated?', 'A. To improve cable installation speed', 'B. To ensure accurate measurement of optical power', 'C. To enhance wireless communication', 'D. To extend cable lifespan', 'B', 'Precise calibration ensures that measurements reflect the true power transmitted through fiber.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which factors can affect the final test results of an Optical Power Meter?', 'A. Surrounding room temperature, connection type, and technician skill', 'B. Cable color, length, and thickness', 'C. Network protocols in use', 'D. Brand of the fiber-optic cable', 'A', 'Environmental and human factors can influence measurement accuracy.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does a Time Domain Reflectometer (TDR) do?', 'A. Issues a signal and measures the bounce back to find faults', 'B. Measures optical power in fiber cables', 'C. Amplifies digital signals', 'D. Converts fiber signals to electrical signals', 'A', 'TDRs detect cable faults and measure distances by analyzing reflected signals.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How does a TDR indicate whether terminators are properly installed and functional?', 'A. By measuring the length of the cable', 'B. By analyzing signal bounce back patterns', 'C. By sending light pulses through fiber', 'D. By measuring resistance', 'B', 'Signal reflections reveal termination status and faults.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary purpose of Optical Time Domain Reflectometers (OTDRs)?', 'A. Measure fiber length and locate faults like breaks and bends', 'B. Amplify fiber-optic signals', 'C. Convert optical signals to electrical signals', 'D. Test network speed', 'A', 'OTDRs provide detailed fiber diagnostics for maintenance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why are OTDR devices considered expensive?', 'A. Because they are made of gold', 'B. Due to their advanced capabilities in measuring and diagnosing fiber networks', 'C. Because they include wireless routers', 'D. Because they require frequent replacement', 'B', 'OTDRs incorporate complex technology for precise fiber analysis.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Test results from Optical Power Meters can be stored and printed or saved to a computer database.', 'A. True', 'B. False', 'C. Only printed, not saved', 'D. Only saved, not printed', 'A', 'Modern OPMs allow result storage and printing for documentation and analysis.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What kind of graphical information might devices like the DTX-1800 depict?', 'A. Attenuation and cross-talk', 'B. Network traffic logs', 'C. User activity patterns', 'D. Signal encryption status', 'A', 'These devices visualize signal loss and interference metrics for troubleshooting.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which device would be most useful for measuring light power transmitted in fiber-optic cables?', 'A. Optical Power Meter', 'B. Time Domain Reflectometer', 'C. Multimeter', 'D. Cable continuity tester', 'A', 'OPMs specifically measure the intensity of light signals in fiber optics.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is coaxial cable composed of?', 'A. Two conductors separated by an insulating spacer', 'B. A single conductor with no insulation', 'C. Multiple twisted pairs of wires', 'D. Fiber optic strands', 'A', 'Coaxial cable has a central conductor and an outer conductor separated by insulation.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the primary use of coaxial cable?', 'A. Transmission of Radio Frequency energy', 'B. Data storage', 'C. Wireless communication', 'D. Power delivery', 'A', 'Coaxial cables are designed to carry RF signals with minimal interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Why does coaxial cable offer excellent EMI control?', 'A. Because of the outer conductor shielding', 'B. Due to its fiber optic core', 'C. Because it is unshielded', 'D. Because it is wireless', 'A', 'The outer conductor acts as a shield protecting against electromagnetic interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which impedance value is primarily used for data signal applications in coaxial cables?', 'A. 50 Ohms', 'B. 75 Ohms', 'C. 100 Ohms', 'D. 25 Ohms', 'A', '50 Ohms impedance is standard for data signal transmission in coaxial cables.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which impedance value is primarily used for video signal applications in coaxial cables?', 'A. 75 Ohms', 'B. 50 Ohms', 'C. 100 Ohms', 'D. 25 Ohms', 'A', '75 Ohms impedance is the standard for video signals in coaxial cables.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What defines a male connector?', 'A. Connector with pins inserted into a female connector', 'B. Connector with holes receiving pins', 'C. Wireless connector', 'D. Connector that does not connect', 'A', 'Male connectors have pins that plug into female connectors.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What defines a female connector?', 'A. Connector with holes to receive pins', 'B. Connector with pins', 'C. Connector that emits wireless signals', 'D. Connector without pins or holes', 'A', 'Female connectors have holes designed to accept male connector pins.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: The power cord connector that plugs into the wall socket is typically a male connector.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'Wall socket connectors typically have pins (male) that fit into female connectors on devices.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which feature of coaxial cable helps maintain tight control over electrical impedance?', 'A. Insulating spacer between conductors', 'B. Twisted pair wires', 'C. Fiber optic core', 'D. Wireless transmission', 'A', 'The insulating spacer ensures consistent impedance along the cable length.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which of the following best describes the outer conductor in coaxial cables?', 'A. Acts as a shield to reduce electromagnetic interference', 'B. Transmits light signals', 'C. Provides power to devices', 'D. Is an uninsulated wire', 'A', 'The outer conductor shields the inner conductor to reduce noise and interference.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What does attenuation (insertion loss) in coaxial cable refer to?', 'A. Loss of power as signal travels through the cable', 'B. Gain of signal strength', 'C. Electrical interference', 'D. Bending radius of the cable', 'A', 'Attenuation means the signal loses power the further it travels.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'How does attenuation in coaxial cable change with frequency?', 'A. Attenuation increases as frequency increases', 'B. Attenuation decreases as frequency increases', 'C. Attenuation stays constant', 'D. Attenuation is unrelated to frequency', 'A', 'Higher frequencies generally suffer more signal loss.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is bend radius in coaxial cable?', 'A. The minimum radius a cable can bend without damage', 'B. The diameter of the cable', 'C. The length of the cable', 'D. The thickness of the insulation', 'A', 'Bend radius defines safe bending limits to avoid damage or performance loss.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the center conductor in a coaxial cable?', 'A. The solid or stranded wire at the center of the cable', 'B. The outer shielding layer', 'C. The insulating material', 'D. The connector tip', 'A', 'The center conductor carries the electrical signal.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is the purpose of a coaxial adapter?', 'A. To convert one connector type or gender to another', 'B. To increase signal strength', 'C. To shield the cable from EMI', 'D. To insulate the cable', 'A', 'Adapters allow connections between different coaxial connectors.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Which material separates the center conductor and the shielding in a coaxial cable?', 'A. Dielectric (insulating material)', 'B. Copper braid', 'C. Plastic sheath', 'D. Metallic foil', 'A', 'Dielectric prevents electrical contact and maintains impedance.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'What is electromagnetic interference (EMI)?', 'A. Electrical or electromagnetic energy that disrupts signals', 'B. A type of connector', 'C. The inner core of a coaxial cable', 'D. A cable insulation material', 'A', 'EMI causes noise and degrades signal quality.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'Frequency is defined as:', 'A. The number of times a periodic event occurs per second (Hertz)', 'B. The length of the cable', 'C. The strength of a signal', 'D. The thickness of a conductor', 'A', 'Frequency measures how often a repeating event happens each second.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'In coaxial cables, impedance measures:', 'A. Resistance to the flow of current (Ohms)', 'B. Cable length', 'C. Frequency', 'D. Connector size', 'A', 'Impedance determines how much the cable resists electrical current flow.', 'medium');
VALUES (4, 'True or False: Coaxial connectors come in many types, such as BNC, SMA, SMB, and F.', 'A. True', 'B. False', '', '', 'A', 'There are various coaxial connector standards designed for different applications.', 'medium');